          "guid": "mt6yf3ae1k1kc2up",
          "message": {
            "text": "Pinned Blink source proves that Network.getResponseBody can transport the exact binary ResourceBuffer after its inspector request is authenticated, but debugger body access is neither the owner-to-resource relation nor CORS authorization. The implementation sequence therefore starts with a private source-truth oracle, gates any stock-CDP join against it, and rejects URL-only or no-CORS body disclosure instead of refetching or weakening browser security.",
            "markdown": "Pinned Blink source proves that Network.getResponseBody can transport the exact binary ResourceBuffer after its inspector request is authenticated, but debugger body access is neither the owner-to-resource relation nor CORS authorization. The implementation sequence therefore starts with a private source-truth oracle, gates any stock-CDP join against it, and rejects URL-only or no-CORS body disclosure instead of refetching or weakening browser security."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "docs/231-authenticated-animated-image-byte-ownership.md"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 9,
                  "endLine": 34,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "Blink retains the exact encoded entity bytes selected for an image in the\nowner's `ImageResourceContent`. Chromium DevTools can return those same bytes\nthrough `Network.getResponseBody(requestId)` because the inspector keeps a weak\nreference to the exact `Resource` and, for binary resources, base64-encodes its\n`ResourceBuffer`. That is the preferred body transport for HTTP(S): it joins\nthe browser's completed response and does not issue a second request.\n\nThe transport is not, by itself, an ownership or security proof. Public CDP\ndoes not expose a general relation from an element, pseudo, or CSS layer to the\n`ImageResourceContent*` it selected, and debugger privilege can read a\ncross-origin no-CORS body that page script could not read. URL equality is\ninsufficient when a URL is redirected, revalidated, intercepted by a service\nworker, reused from memory cache, requested more than once, or retained by a\nstale owner.\n\nThe strict design therefore has two independent requirements:\n\n1. authenticate the exact owner/slot -> `ImageResourceContent` -> `Resource`\n   -> inspector request relation before and after body collection; and\n2. authorize byte release from Blink's actual origin-clean/CORS result, never\n   from CDP body availability, response headers alone, or an independent\n   refetch.\n\nA pinned renderer-side truth helper is required as the investigation oracle\nfor that relation. A stock-CDP implementation may ship only the subset whose\npublic join is proven equivalent by the oracle. Every ambiguous route remains"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "tags": [
              "rationale"
            ],
